#!/usr/bin/env bash
set -euo pipefail
#
# Interactive Nushell startup budget.
#
# This repo put five generated autoload hooks and three nu_scripts completers in front of
# a shell that used to be `nu` and nothing else. Every one of them was justified on its
# own and none of them is the problem; the sum is. Nothing else in the tree measures the
# sum, so this is the gate that keeps it honest — a convenience that costs 3 ms now has to
# be argued for against a number instead of waved through.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# What is measured, and why it is not `nu -c exit`
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# `nu -c exit` measures none of it. As of Nushell 0.116 `nu -c` and `nu script.nu` load
# neither `env.nu` nor `config.nu` nor `$nu.data-dir/vendor/autoload` — `nu -c
# '$env.config.edit_mode'` answers `emacs`, not the `vi` that config.nu sets. A benchmark
# built on it would be blind to every file this gate exists to protect.
#
# The pinned method is therefore a replay: `nu -n -c` with `env.nu`, `config.nu` and every
# `.nu` of the *built* vendor/autoload directory sourced explicitly, in the filename order
# Nushell sources them, against a `nu -n -c ""` floor measured on the same machine.
#
# The alternative, a real pty — `script -qec "nu -i" /dev/null` — is the more faithful
# shape and was rejected for the gate anyway:
#
#   * it needs a tty and an *activated* machine, because vendor/autoload only exists under
#     $HOME after a rebuild. A runner has neither, and so does a host whose first switch
#     is what the pull request is asking for. The gate has to answer before the switch.
#   * it folds reedline construction and terminal probing into the number. Neither is ours
#     and neither is what a change under features/cli/* can regress, so they are noise
#     with a machine-dependent mean sitting on top of the signal.
#   * on any host with the auto-attach guard on, it hands the benchmark to zellij.
#
# What the replay cannot see is the work behind `$nu.is-interactive`: the Hávamál stanza
# (~3 ms) and the zellij auto-attach. That is a deliberate exclusion — a random file read
# does not belong in a benchmark, and an attach even less so. HAVAMAL=0 and
# ZELLIJ_AUTO_ATTACH=0 are exported below regardless: the guards already answer false for
# `-c`, and this is the gate that runs against the next Nushell that changes its mind.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Two verdicts: this change against its base, and the sum against a hard backstop
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Until GH-217 the gate was one number: startup/floor from a single hyperfine run, failing
# above 4.0x. The idea was that both halves move with the machine. They do not move
# together. 90 GitHub runs on trees that did not touch the shell (2026-11-21/22) gave:
#
#   ratio   min 2.85   p10 3.06   median 3.39   p90 3.88   max 4.13   (sd 0.29)
#
# and the spread is between runners, not within a job: runners with a 6-8 ms floor gave
# 3.7-4.1x, runners with a 10-14 ms floor 3.2-3.4x, and every red was a ~7.3 ms-floor
# runner (4.03x, 4.05x, 4.13x). `nu -n` is mostly exec and dynamic loading; the replay is
# mostly parsing and evaluation; a different CPU weighs them differently. Within one job
# the means are good to a few hundredths of a millisecond, so taking more samples there
# does not help, and one completer (+3-7% of startup) is smaller than the runner-to-runner
# spread, so no single ratio threshold can be both quiet and strict.
#
# So the gate compares like with like:
#
#   1. The differential, the real gate. The *base* — the commit this change lands on: the
#      merge commit's first parent on a pull request, the previous main commit on main —
#      is realised next to this tree and replayed on the same machine, paired: every
#      iteration times the floor, this tree and the base back to back. What is compared is
#      the startup *excess* over the floor, the part this repo owns:
#      1 + median(this - base) / median(base - floor). Above 1 + DIFF_BUDGET it fails.
#      Measured on a loaded drakkar (load 6-7): an unchanged tree read +0.2%, +1.2% and
#      +0.3%; one more completer (cargo, +1.3 ms in the completer table) read +6.3% and
#      failed. The cheapest completer, make (+0.5 ms), is about the budget itself.
#
#   2. The backstop, for what no single change shows. A dozen changes each under
#      DIFF_BUDGET compound into one that no differential sees, so the absolute ratio still
#      fails above HARD_RATIO. That is set above the noise the 90 runs measured — their max
#      is 4.13x and mean + 3.5 sd is 4.42x — so it stays quiet on an unchanged tree on any
#      runner seen so far and still catches the sum drifting up by about a third.
#
#   3. The trend, at the old line. Above WARN_RATIO (4.0x, the original budget) the step
#      summary and the log say so without failing: on a fast runner that is noise, on every
#      runner it is the sum asking to be looked at.
#
# Measured on drakkar, 2026-11-05, hyperfine 2.0.0 / nushell 0.116.1, 100-350 runs each:
#
#   floor      `nu -n -c ""`                            8.3 ms  ± 0.7
#   + env.nu + config.nu                               11.6 ms  ± 0.7
#   + the five generated hooks (00-40)                 21.6 ms  ± 0.8
#   + the three nu_scripts completers (52-54)          27.4 ms  ± 1.1   = 3.32x floor
#
# Re-derive any of it with SHELL_BUDGET_AUTOLOAD (below) rather than trusting this comment.
#

ROOT="$(cd "$(dirname "$0")/../.." && pwd)"
cd "$ROOT"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The budgets. One declared place; everything below reads them and nothing restates them.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
DIFF_BUDGET=0.025 # this change's startup excess over its base's, at most +2.5%
HARD_RATIO=4.5    # startup/floor, the backstop: above the 90-run noise (max 4.13x)
WARN_RATIO=4.0    # startup/floor, the trend line: reported, not failed

# Sampling. The ratio: one hyperfine run, as before GH-217 (--min-runs is a floor for the
# slow benchmarks; the floor gets 300+ runs out of hyperfine's default minimum wall time).
# The differential: PAIRS paired iterations of floor, this tree and the base, after
# PAIR_WARMUP untimed ones.
WARMUP=5
MIN_RUNS=50
PAIRS="${SHELL_BUDGET_PAIRS:-150}"
PAIR_WARMUP=5

# Whose vendor/autoload is measured. Every host takes features/cli/shell-init with its
# default hooks and completers, so this picks a machine to evaluate, not a configuration.
HOST="${SHELL_BUDGET_HOST:-drakkar}"

# Where the raw hyperfine JSON lands (one file per round), so a regression can be read as
# a trend rather than a bare pass/fail. .tmp is gitignored; on a runner the step summary
# below carries the same fields into the run's permanent record.
REPORT_DIR="${SHELL_BUDGET_REPORT_DIR:-$ROOT/.tmp/shell-budget}"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Toolchain
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# `nix-shell -p hyperfine nushell` would resolve against the *channel* — nixos-unstable on
# a runner (.github/actions/setup-nix pins nix_path to it), and possibly nothing at all on
# a host with no channels. A different nushell is a different startup cost, so the one
# measured has to be the one this flake pins: `--inputs-from .` resolves `nixpkgs#` against
# this flake's locked input. `devenv shell` stays out for the reason bin/ci/assay gives.
if [[ -z "${SHELL_BUDGET_TOOLCHAIN:-}" ]]; then
  export SHELL_BUDGET_TOOLCHAIN=1
  exec nix shell --inputs-from . nixpkgs#hyperfine nixpkgs#nushell nixpkgs#jq -c "$0" "$@"
fi

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The base
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# SHELL_BUDGET_BASE names the commit to compare against. CI sets it to HEAD^1 (checkout
# fetch-depth 2): on a pull request HEAD is GitHub's merge commit and its first parent is
# the base branch it merges into, which is also right for a branch that was rebased; on a
# push to main it is the previous main commit. In CI it must be set and must resolve —
# a gate that quietly dropped its comparison would pass everything. Locally it defaults
# to the merge-base with origin/main; SHELL_BUDGET_BASE=none measures this tree alone
# (the backstop and the trend still apply).
base="${SHELL_BUDGET_BASE:-}"
base_rev=""
if [[ -z "$base" ]]; then
  if [[ "${GITHUB_ACTIONS:-}" == true ]]; then
    echo "shell-budget: SHELL_BUDGET_BASE is not set; CI must name the commit to compare against" >&2
    exit 1
  fi
  base="$(git merge-base HEAD origin/main 2>/dev/null || echo none)"
fi
if [[ "$base" != none ]]; then
  if ! base_rev="$(git rev-parse --verify --quiet "$base^{commit}")"; then
    echo "shell-budget: the base '$base' is not a commit in this clone (in CI: is fetch-depth 2?)" >&2
    exit 1
  fi
fi

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The autoload directory under measurement
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# features/cli/shell-init keeps its derivation private — the module only ever mentions it
# inside the activation script's text — so there is no `nix build .#something` for it. The
# string context is the handle: a writeText holding that activation text has the autoload
# directory as a build input, so realising the one realises the other, and the path can
# then be read back out of the file.
#
# The `sessionVariables` override is what keeps that affordable. NixOS declares
# NAUTILUS_4_EXTENSION_DIR as "${config.system.path}/lib/nautilus/extensions-4" and
# GIO_EXTRA_MODULES as three gio module directories, so `00-nixos-env.nu` carries the
# host's *entire* systemPackages closure as string context — nix will not write that file
# until every package on the machine exists. Realising it verbatim costs a desktop closure
# (on drakkar: ollama-cuda, a full nix-index database, the lot), which is not something a
# pull request gate gets to download. Re-declaring each variable as its own value with the
# context discarded produces a byte-identical file — same names, same values, same count of
# conditional assignments, and the cost of `$env.FOO = "/nix/store/..."` does not depend on
# whether that path has been built — off a derivation that depends on nothing but text.
# Verified by `diff -r` against the real directory before this was pinned; the only
# remaining runtime reference of the measured directory is starship.
#
# The base is the same expression against `git+file:<this clone>?rev=<base>`: nix reads the
# commit out of the clone's object store, so there is no second checkout to make or clean.
#
# SHELL_BUDGET_AUTOLOAD skips all of that for this tree and measures a directory of `.nu`
# files given directly. That is how you price a completer before adding it to the feature,
# and how you check that this gate still bites: copy the built directory, drop another
# completer from the nu_scripts input into the copy, and point this at it.
realise() { # <flake ref> <out file>: the autoload directory's path into <out file>
  local ref
  ref="$(nix build --no-link --print-out-paths --impure --expr "
    let
      flake = builtins.getFlake \"$1\";
      lib = flake.inputs.nixpkgs.lib;
      host = flake.nixosConfigurations.${HOST};
      # A sessionVariables value is a string or a list of them; NixOS joins the list with
      # \":\" when it renders the file, so each element is stripped on its own.
      bare = v:
        if v == null
        then null
        else if builtins.isList v
        then map (x: builtins.unsafeDiscardStringContext (toString x)) v
        else builtins.unsafeDiscardStringContext (toString v);
      measured = host.extendModules {
        modules = [
          {
            # mkOverride 10 rather than mkForce: a feature that already forces one of these
            # would otherwise collide with this definition at the same priority.
            environment.sessionVariables =
              lib.mapAttrs (_: v: lib.mkOverride 10 (bare v))
                host.config.environment.sessionVariables;
          }
        ];
      };
    in
      measured.pkgs.writeText \"nushell-vendor-autoload-ref\"
        measured.config.vfiles.legacy.nushellVendorAutoload.text
  ")"
  grep -oE '/nix/store/[a-z0-9]{32}-nushell-vendor-autoload' "$ref" | head -n1 >"$2"
}

work="$(mktemp -d)"
trap 'rm -rf -- "$work"' EXIT

# Both realisations at once: each is one evaluation of a whole host, and they share nothing
# but the store, so the base costs the job its wall time only once.
pids=()
if [[ -n "${SHELL_BUDGET_AUTOLOAD:-}" ]]; then
  echo "shell-budget: measuring the autoload directory given in SHELL_BUDGET_AUTOLOAD"
  printf '%s\n' "$SHELL_BUDGET_AUTOLOAD" >"$work/head.path"
else
  echo "shell-budget: realising nixosConfigurations.${HOST}'s vendor/autoload for this tree..."
  realise "$ROOT" "$work/head.path" &
  pids+=("$!")
fi
if [[ "$base" != none ]]; then
  echo "shell-budget: ...and for the base, ${base_rev:0:12}"
  realise "git+file://$ROOT?rev=$base_rev&shallow=1" "$work/base.path" &
  pids+=("$!")
fi
for pid in "${pids[@]}"; do
  wait "$pid" || {
    echo "shell-budget: realising an autoload directory failed (above)" >&2
    exit 1
  }
done

# <name> <autoload dir> <nushell dir>: the replay command for one tree into $work/<name>.cmd
replay_of() {
  local dir="$2" files
  if [[ ! -d "$dir" ]]; then
    echo "shell-budget: $1: not a directory: ${dir:-<empty>}" >&2
    exit 1
  fi
  files=("$dir"/*.nu)
  if [[ ! -e "${files[0]}" ]]; then
    echo "shell-budget: $1: no .nu files in $dir" >&2
    exit 1
  fi
  local replay="source '$3/env.nu'; source '$3/config.nu'; "
  for f in "${files[@]}"; do
    replay+="source '$f'; "
  done
  printf '%s' "$replay" >"$work/$1.cmd"
  printf '%s' "${#files[@]}" >"$work/$1.files"
}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Measure
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# A throwaway HOME, so the number does not depend on whose machine this is: env.nu builds
# PATH out of $env.HOME at load, and 30-carapace.nu puts $HOME/.config/carapace/bin on it.
# XDG_DATA_HOME goes with it, which keeps `nu` away from the real vendor/autoload — `-n`
# already does that, but the guard costs nothing and the files here are the point.
export HOME="$work/home"
mkdir -p "$HOME"
export XDG_DATA_HOME="$HOME/.local/share"
export ZELLIJ_AUTO_ATTACH=0
export HAVAMAL=0
unset ZELLIJ TMUX NU_LOG_LEVEL || true

replay_of head "$(cat "$work/head.path")" "$ROOT/features/cli/nushell"
names=(floor head)
if [[ "$base" != none ]]; then
  # The base's env.nu and config.nu are the base's own, with the files they source next to
  # them (config.nu sources havamal.nu by a relative path): the directory, from the commit.
  mkdir -p "$work/base-tree"
  git archive "$base_rev" features/cli/nushell | tar -x -C "$work/base-tree"
  replay_of base "$(cat "$work/base.path")" "$work/base-tree/features/cli/nushell"
  names+=(base)
fi

# One run of each, outside hyperfine, so a replay that errors fails here with its error
# visible rather than as a mysteriously fast benchmark. Nushell exits 0 on a `source` error.
for n in "${names[@]:1}"; do
  if ! out="$(nu -n -c "$(cat "$work/$n.cmd") print 'shell-budget-replay-ok'" 2>&1)" \
    || [[ "$out" != *shell-budget-replay-ok* ]]; then
    echo "shell-budget: the $n startup replay does not run clean:" >&2
    echo "$out" >&2
    exit 1
  fi
done

rm -rf -- "$REPORT_DIR"
mkdir -p "$REPORT_DIR"

# The ratio, the backstop's and the trend's number: exactly the pre-GH-217 measurement (one
# hyperfine run, means), so the 90 runs the backstop was set from stay comparable. The base
# rides along for the report.
args=(-n floor 'nu -n -c ""')
for n in "${names[@]:1}"; do
  args+=(-n "$n" "nu -n -c \"$(cat "$work/$n.cmd")\"")
done
hyperfine --warmup "$WARMUP" --min-runs "$MIN_RUNS" --style basic \
  --export-json "$REPORT_DIR/ratio.json" "${args[@]}"

# The differential, paired. hyperfine times each command as one contiguous block, and a
# slow second of the machine landing on one block and not the other moved an unchanged
# tree by +-5% on a loaded drakkar. Here every iteration times floor, this tree and the
# base back to back, the order rotated each iteration, and the verdict reads the median of
# the per-iteration differences: a slow stretch lands on all three alike. The clock is
# bash's EPOCHREALTIME, no fork; the exec overhead it includes is in floor, this tree and
# the base alike, and cancels out of the excess.
if [[ "$base" != none ]]; then
  declare -A cmd=([floor]="" [head]="$(cat "$work/head.cmd")" [base]="$(cat "$work/base.cmd")")
  order=(floor head base)
  declare -A took
  for ((i = 0; i < PAIR_WARMUP + PAIRS; i++)); do
    for ((j = 0; j < 3; j++)); do
      n="${order[$(((i + j) % 3))]}"
      t0="${EPOCHREALTIME/./}"
      nu -n -c "${cmd[$n]}" >/dev/null
      t1="${EPOCHREALTIME/./}"
      took[$n]=$((t1 - t0))
    done
    ((i < PAIR_WARMUP)) || printf '%d\t%d\t%d\n' "${took[floor]}" "${took[head]}" "${took[base]}"
  done >"$REPORT_DIR/pairs.tsv"
fi

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Report and verdict
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
if [[ "$(jq -r '.schema_version' "$REPORT_DIR/ratio.json")" != "2" ]]; then
  echo "shell-budget: unexpected hyperfine JSON schema; the field names below moved" >&2
  exit 1
fi

IFS=$'\t' read -r floor_ms head_ms base_ms ratio base_ratio < <(
  jq -r '
    def wall($n): (.results[] | select(.name == $n) | .summary.time_wall_clock);
    def has($n): any(.results[]; .name == $n);
    [ (wall("floor") | .mean * 1000),
      (wall("head") | .mean * 1000),
      (if has("base") then (wall("base") | .mean * 1000) else -1 end),
      ((wall("head") | .mean) / (wall("floor") | .mean)),
      (if has("base") then ((wall("base") | .mean) / (wall("floor") | .mean)) else -1 end)
    ] | @tsv
  ' "$REPORT_DIR/ratio.json"
)

# median of column <n> of pairs.tsv after <awk expression over $1 floor, $2 head, $3 base>
median_of() {
  awk -F'\t' "{ print $1 }" "$REPORT_DIR/pairs.tsv" | sort -g \
    | awk '{ a[NR] = $1 } END { if (NR == 0) exit 1; print (NR % 2) ? a[(NR + 1) / 2] : (a[NR / 2] + a[NR / 2 + 1]) / 2 }'
}
diff=-1
if [[ "$base" != none ]]; then
  # 1 + (this tree - base) / (base - floor), each a median of per-iteration differences.
  diff="$(awk -v d="$(median_of '$2 - $3')" -v e="$(median_of '$3 - $1')" 'BEGIN { printf "%.4f", 1 + d / e }')"
fi

if [[ -z "${ratio:-}" || -z "${diff:-}" ]]; then
  echo "shell-budget: could not read the timings back out of $REPORT_DIR" >&2
  exit 1
fi

files="$(cat "$work/head.files")"
printf '\nshell-budget: nushell interactive startup, replayed (differential: %d paired iterations)\n' "$PAIRS"
printf '  nushell    %s\n' "$(nu --version)"
printf '  autoload   %s (%d files)\n' "$(cat "$work/head.path")" "$files"
printf '  floor      %6.2f ms  nu -n -c ""\n' "$floor_ms"
printf '  startup    %6.2f ms  env.nu + config.nu + autoload   ratio %.2fx  (backstop %.2fx, trend line %.2fx)\n' \
  "$head_ms" "$ratio" "$HARD_RATIO" "$WARN_RATIO"
if [[ "$base" != none ]]; then
  printf '  base       %6.2f ms  %s   ratio %.2fx\n' "$base_ms" "${base_rev:0:12}" "$base_ratio"
  printf '  change     %+6.1f%%   of the startup excess over the floor, against the base (budget +%.1f%%)\n' \
    "$(awk -v d="$diff" 'BEGIN { print (d - 1) * 100 }')" "$(awk -v b="$DIFF_BUDGET" 'BEGIN { print b * 100 }')"
fi

# One greppable line per run: a sequence of these across CI logs is the trend. The fields
# up to files= are the pre-GH-217 line's, so old and new logs read as one series.
record="$(printf 'shell-budget\thost=%s\tfloor_ms=%.2f\tstartup_ms=%.2f\tratio=%.3f\tbudget=%.2f\tfiles=%d\tbase=%s\tbase_ratio=%.3f\tdiff=%.4f\tpairs=%d' \
  "$HOST" "$floor_ms" "$head_ms" "$ratio" "$HARD_RATIO" "$files" "${base_rev:0:12}" "$base_ratio" "$diff" "$PAIRS")"
[[ "$base" != none ]] || record="${record/base=	/base=none	}"
printf '%s\n' "$record"
if [[ -n "${GITHUB_STEP_SUMMARY:-}" ]]; then
  {
    printf '### Nushell startup budget\n\n'
    printf '| floor | startup | ratio | base ratio | change vs base | budgets |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n'
    printf '| %.2f ms | %.2f ms | %.2fx | %.2fx | %+.1f%% | +%.1f%%, %.1fx hard, %.1fx trend |\n\n' \
      "$floor_ms" "$head_ms" "$ratio" "$base_ratio" "$(awk -v d="$diff" 'BEGIN { print (d - 1) * 100 }')" \
      "$(awk -v b="$DIFF_BUDGET" 'BEGIN { print b * 100 }')" "$HARD_RATIO" "$WARN_RATIO"
    printf '<pre>%s</pre>\n' "$record"
  } >>"$GITHUB_STEP_SUMMARY"
fi

over() { awk -v a="$1" -v b="$2" 'BEGIN { exit !(a > b) }'; }
failed=0

if [[ "$base" != none ]] && over "$diff" "$(awk -v b="$DIFF_BUDGET" 'BEGIN { print 1 + b }')"; then
  failed=1
  cat >&2 <<EOF

shell-budget: FAIL — this change adds $(awk -v d="$diff" 'BEGIN { printf "%.1f", (d - 1) * 100 }')% to the
interactive startup's cost over the \`nu -n\` floor, measured against its base ${base_rev:0:12}
on this machine; the budget is +$(awk -v b="$DIFF_BUDGET" 'BEGIN { print b * 100 }')%. That is about $(awk \
  -v h="$head_ms" -v b="$base_ms" 'BEGIN { printf "%.1f", h - b }') ms of this machine's time.
EOF
fi

if over "$ratio" "$HARD_RATIO"; then
  failed=1
  cat >&2 <<EOF

shell-budget: FAIL — interactive startup is $(printf '%.2f' "$ratio")x the \`nu -n\` floor on this
machine, over the ${HARD_RATIO}x backstop. No single change has to be over its own budget for
this: the sum is, and something has to give it back.
EOF
elif over "$ratio" "$WARN_RATIO"; then
  msg="startup is $(printf '%.2f' "$ratio")x the nu -n floor here, over the ${WARN_RATIO}x trend line (not a failure; the backstop is ${HARD_RATIO}x)"
  printf '\nshell-budget: note — %s\n' "$msg"
  [[ "${GITHUB_ACTIONS:-}" != true ]] || printf '::warning title=shell-budget::%s\n' "$msg"
fi

if ((failed)); then
  cat >&2 <<EOF

The startup path is features/cli/nushell/{env.nu,config.nu} plus every file
features/cli/shell-init generates into vendor/autoload. To find the cost, bisect it:
hyperfine \`nu -n -c "source FILE"\` against \`nu -n -c ""\` one file at a time, or re-run
this script with SHELL_BUDGET_AUTOLOAD pointed at a directory holding a subset of them.

cli.shellInit.completers is the knob meant to be turned down; a hook is not.
EOF
  exit 1
fi
printf '\nshell-budget: OK\n'
